tind-wireguard
==============

What it does
  Gives every Tind device an always-on, split-tunnel WireGuard connection to the
  servr.no server. Only the server's own subnet and names under servr.no use the
  tunnel; everything else (including Proton VPN) is unaffected.

Per-device steps
  1. sudo tind-enroll init            -> prints this device's PUBLIC key
  2. send the public key to the server admin; the admin replies with an enrolment block
     (ADDRESS, SERVER_ENDPOINT, SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY, ALLOWED_IPS, DNS_SERVER, optionally LAN_ENDPOINT)
  3. sudo tind-enroll apply           -> paste the block, end with Ctrl-D (or: apply FILE)
  4. tind-enroll status

Design rules
  - Each device makes its own key. No private key is ever in the Tind image.
  - The image contains no server details at all: no endpoint, no server key, no subnet.
    They arrive in the enrolment block, so nothing about the server is in the repository.
  - The block is parsed as data, never run as shell; every value is checked before use.
  - ALLOWED_IPS must never contain 0.0.0.0/0 (tind-enroll refuses).
  - Send the block over a channel you trust: whoever controls DNS_SERVER controls what
    names under servr.no resolve to while the tunnel is up.
  - Server side: docs/server/tind-add-peer and docs/servr-wireguard-server.md in the Tind repository.

Which address the tunnel uses
  At home the tunnel must reach the server on its LAN address, not through the router (the router
  cannot carry gigabit LAN traffic). tind-enroll refresh picks the address and writes it into the
  NetworkManager profile, so NetworkManager never looks the name up itself (its lookup would go
  through the tunnel's own DNS and give the public address).
  1. LAN_ENDPOINT (optional, /etc/tind/wireguard/lan-endpoint): tried once on every new network
     (told apart by the gateway's MAC address). The running tunnel is pointed at it and the server's
     tunnel address is pinged; only the real server can answer inside the tunnel, so a reply proves
     the path. Kept if it answers; tried again after 30 minutes, or when the tunnel goes quiet.
  2. Otherwise SERVER_ENDPOINT (/etc/tind/wireguard/endpoint), looked up only through the DNS of the
     network the device is on: the public address away from home.
  By hand: sudo tind-enroll refresh. Check: journalctl -t tind-enroll

Watchdog
  tind-wireguard-watchdog.timer runs every minute (first run 45 seconds after boot). It runs
  tind-enroll refresh, which follows the device between networks and reconnects when the address
  has to change (another network, a new home WAN IP). When the last handshake is older than 150 seconds it takes the connection
  down and up again. Check: journalctl -t tind-wireguard-watchdog
